  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA quiet Thanksgiving continues across the Northland for the \nrest of the day, with no weather expected as a surface ridge \naxis stretches across Lake Superior and the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley. Skies remain mostly clear aside from some high \nclouds associated with a large low pressure system moving up the\nEast Coast. Over northwest Wisconsin, lingering stratus will \ncontinue to break up with daytime mixing this afternoon, only to\nfill back in toward the northwest tonight. Residual low-level \nmoisture trapped beneath this cloud deck from the southeastern \nU.S. system could lead to patchy fog and areas of freezing fog \novernight as temperatures drop, potentially causing localized \nreduced visibilities and slick spots.\n\nFriday will be dry and quiet once again for most of the day before a\ncold front from a low pressure system in Ontario sweeps across the\narea during the afternoon and evening. As frontal moisture battles\ndry air to the west, very little if any precipitation is expected to\nreach the ground during the afternoon from north central Minnesota to\nthe Interstate 35 and Highway 53 corridor. While some dry air will\nstill need to be overcome in Wisconsin and Minnesota Arrowhead,\nthe lingering low-level moisture in place will help \nprecipitation reach the ground more readily there than in most\nof Minnesota. Exact timing of the cold front and surface \ntemperatures will be key to determining precipitation types \nFriday evening and overnight in northwest Wisconsin and the\nArrowhead. Model soundings indicate warm air remaining aloft \ninto the early overnight hours as surface temperatures lower, \nwhich is a classic setup for freezing rain or freezing drizzle \nif precipitation develops. However, some soundings keep surface \ntemperatures just above freezing, which would result in all \nrain. Regardless of precipitation type, overall moisture with \nthis system is very limited, so any ice or snow accumulations \nwill be little to none.\n\nHeading into Saturday and Sunday, the low pressure system in Ontario\nwill continue to sit and spin just north of the Canadian border.\nOngoing cold air advection paired with small amounts of moisture will\nlead to additional chances for a light wintry mix throughout the\nweekend. Precipitation type will depend largely on the time of day,\nwith rain or a wintry mix favored during the warmer daytime hours and\nlight snow at night. Overall amounts will remain very light with\nminimal travel impacts expected across the area. Cold air moving over\nLake Superior will also generate lake effect snow showers, though\nmost of that snow will fall across the Upper Peninsula of Michigan.\nBehind the system, blustery west to northwest winds will develop\nSaturday into Sunday, creating breezy conditions over the\nNorthland.\n\nBy early next week, the upper-level pattern shifts to a quasi-zonal\nflow across the Upper Midwest, allowing a train of weak shortwaves to\nmove through the Northland on Monday and Tuesday. Monday looks mostly\nquiet before hit-or-miss chances for light wintry precipitation\nreturn Monday night into Tuesday. Moisture remains limited with these\npassing disturbances, keeping any accumulations light and preventing\nsignificant travel disruptions.\n\nThe low-impact pattern persists into Wednesday as another weak wave\npasses across the region, bringing additional slight chances for\nlight rain and snow. No large storm systems are signaled in model\nguidance through the middle of next week, and temperatures across the\nNorthland are expected to hover near to slightly above seasonal\naverages.",
